⚠ PRE-FLIGHT REQUIRED before any code (9-point — extended from Batch 15 lesson):
1. Run `git status` + `git log -1` to confirm working tree state matches expectations.
2. Confirm `pnpm vitest run` → 651/651 GREEN baseline (or document deviation if no source
   drift since last GREEN, like Batches 13/14/15 did).
3. Read the input Zod schema of any router procedure that .whatsnext claims will be reused —
   this catches scope mismatches BEFORE the BUILD BATCH PROPOSAL stage (Batch 12 lesson).
4. If a router change in scope newly READS a field from the existing record (e.g. a gate or
   conditional), verify all tests hitting that code path mock that field (Batch 13 lesson).
5. Grep schema.prisma for any model referenced in the proposed scope; verify presence and
   field types before scoping migrations. (Batch 14 paid for itself here — proposed "new
   EcommerceOrder entity" was already in init migration.)
6. Confirm package.json contains any third-party library called out in the proposed scope.
7. Re-read .claude/rules/memory-governance.md §1 (Tiered Decomposition) for this batch's tier.
8. ⚠ Emit explicit branch-creation line item in the BUILD BATCH PROPOSAL so user can catch
   silent direct-to-main slips at confirm time. Example:
   "Branch feat/batch-16-item-1-{slug} will be created before any file write."
9. ⚠ NEW from Batch 15 — when proposal text says "Server Component consuming X.Y procedure"
   for a PUBLIC route, grep the router for `X.Y: publicProcedure` vs `protectedProcedure`.
   Mismatch means either (a) pivot to direct Prisma in the page, or (b) split the procedure
   into public + protected variants. Item 3 of Batch 15 was caught at scope-pivot time;
   adding this check moves the catch upstream to pre-flight.

